# Build OUTPUT only.
#
# This used to read `*/payload/`, which was right when every example was a
# `native` package: the payload was a compiled binary produced by the example's
# Makefile, and committing it would have been committing a build product.
#
# Then the `portable` application type arrived, and for a portable package the
# payload IS THE SOURCE — that is the entire point of the type. The old rule
# therefore excluded the portable example's `payload/src/` from the repository,
# so a fresh clone had no portable example at all and
# tests/acceptance/05_portable_compile.sh — the suite that proves the portable
# capability — could not run. It passed only on machines where those files
# happened to exist untracked in the working tree.
#
# So: ignore compiled output, by the directory compiled output goes in, and never
# a whole payload tree.
#
# `**/` matters and is not decoration: a gitignore pattern that CONTAINS a slash
# is anchored to this directory, so a bare `payload/bin/` would only match
# `examples/payload/bin/` and nothing else. The examples are grouped by kind now,
# so a payload is two levels down (`native/gui-hello/payload/bin/`) -- both
# `payload/bin/` and `*/payload/bin/` silently match nothing there.
#
# tests/acceptance/00_repository.sh asserts both halves: that every example a test
# depends on is IN the repository, and that no compiled payload is.
**/payload/bin/
**/payload/lib/
